      "text": "Let $P(x)$ denote the given equation or $f(x)+f(1-\\frac1x)=\\log|x|$, I am just going to prove that $f(x)=k\\log|x|$ for some constant $k$\nWe have P(x) $\\iff$ $f(x)=f\\left(1-\\frac1x\\right)$ thus we have mapped 2 points per value. This in turn means that the image of y can only be $D=\\{+\\log, -\\log, \\frac{\\log}{x}\\ \\forall x \\in \\mathbb{Q}^{+}\\}$ \nWe are going to stick to the assumption that $f(x)=\\log|x|$ so this will not give any contradiction. Let Q(x) denote $Q(x)=\\frac{\\log(x)}{x}=\\log^{2}(x)$\nWe just have to show that that the points lie on a circle, thus we have $\\log(x )+\\log\\left(1-\\frac1x \\right)=0 \\implies 1=P(x)$\nHence: $P(x)$ : $\\left(\\frac{\\log(2007)}{2007},\\frac{\\log(2005.969)}{2005.969}\\right) $ so $f(2007)=\\boxed{\\log(2007)}$\nThe solution was direct.  Unnecessary solving for $k$'s in the equation and evaluating it for the answer should take [the same amount of time as this, hence the immensity of this solution]\n\nThis is a detailing of dxum067's solution.",
