      "text": "The equation is given to be\n\\begin{align*}\n f(x) + f\\left( 1 - \\frac{1}{x} \\right) &= \\log |x|, \\\\\n f\\left( 1 - \\frac{1}{x}\\right) &= \\log |x| - f(x)\n\\end{align*}\nSince $f$ is now expressible through substitution, we try to put $x = 1/2$.  Then $1 - 1/x = 2$.  After plugging in, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n f(f(1/2)) &= f(-1/2) + f(2), \\\\\n f(2) &= f(-1/2) + f(f(1/2)) \n\\end{align*}\nGetting rid of $f(2)$, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n f(f(1/2)) - f(-1/2) &= f(-1/2) + f(f(1/2)), \\\\\n f(f(1/2)) - 2 f(f(1/2)) &= f(-1/2) - f(-1/2), \\\\\n 0 &= f(-1/2)\n\\end{align*}\nTherefore, substituting back into our previous equation gives\n\\begin{align*}\nf(f(1/2)) &= f(-1/2) + f(2), \\\\\nf(-1/2) &= f(-1/2) + f(2), \\\\\n0&= f(2)\n\\end{align*}\nTherefore\n\\begin{align*}\nf(-1/2) &= f(2) = 0\n\\end{align*}\nAnd thus, $0=f(-1/2)=f(2)$, meaning $\\boxed{f(2007)=\\log(|2007|)}$, which we can format with $f(2007) = \\log 2007$. The answer is \\log(2007)",
