      "text": "To find $f(2007)$, we first note the given property:\n\n\\[\nf\\left(x\\right) + f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\log\\lvert x\\rvert.\n\\]\n\nSince $x = \\frac{n}{k} \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ with $\\gcd(n,k) = 1$ (meaning the elements aren't 0 or 1), and $x,1-x,1+\\frac{1}{x},1-\\frac{1}{x} \\in A$. Thus, there exist $f(x),f(1-x),f(1+\\frac{1}{x}),f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\in \\mathbb{R}$.  \nHence, this makes $f(x),f(1-x),f(1+\\frac{1}{x}),f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x}\\right)$ we can get.\n\nLet's replace $x$ with $1 - \\frac{1}{x}$ in the functional equation:\n\n\\[\nf\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x}\\right) + f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{x}}\\right) = \\log 1.\n\\]\n\nSince $\\log 1 = 0$, simply replace the term in the numerator. In our imagination, just simplify,\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x}\\right) + f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{x}}\\right) &= \\log 1\\\\\nf\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x}\\right) + f\\left(2x-1\\right) &= 0 & \\text{Simplify }\n\\end{align*}\n\nSince $f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x}\\right) = -f\\left(2x-1\\right)$, then we can plug in:\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf\\left(x\\right) + f\\left(2x-1\\right) &= \\log\\lvert x\\rvert& \\text{By our functional equation.}\n\\end{align*}\n\nAdd $f\\left(2x - 1\\right) + f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)$ and then $f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)$ we have\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf\\left(2x-1\\right) + f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x+1}\\right) + f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x+1}\\right) &= \\log\\lvert x\\rvert + \\log\\lvert 2x-1\\rvert\\\\\n2 f\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x+1}\\right) &= \\log\\lvert x\\rvert + \\log\\lvert 2x-1\\rvert\\\\\nf\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x+1}\\right) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\left[ \\log\\lvert x\\rvert+\\log\\lvert 2x-1\\rvert \\right]\\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\log\\lvert x(2x-1)\\rvert.\n\\end{align*}\n\nIf we replace $x$ with $2007$ we get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2007+1}\\right) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\log\\lvert 2007(2006)\\rvert \\\\\nf(2006) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\log\\lvert 2007(2006)\\rvert\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, if we replace $x$ with $2006$ instead, we get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2006}\\right) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\log\\left(2007(2006-1)\\right)\\\\\nf\\left(\\frac{1}{2006}\\right) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\log\\lvert 2007^2 - 2006\\rvert\n\\end{align*}\n\nWe know that if we replace $x$ with $2006$ in the original equation,\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf\\left(2006\\right) & +f\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2006}\\right) = \\log\\lvert 2006\\rvert\\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} \\log\\lvert 2007^2 - 2006\\rvert &= \\frac{1}{2}\\log\\lvert 2006\\rvert + \\log\\lvert 2006\\rvert\\\\\n\\lvert 2007^2 - 2006\\rvert &= 4012 \\lvert 2006\\rvert\\\\\n2007^2 - 2006 &= 4012 \\times 2006\\\\\nx^2 - y &= 4kx\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow back to $x$ being $2007$, so $x^{2} - y = 4kx$. Considering this quadratic equation, it's clear to you that $y$ must be $2007^{2}$. As it's clear to see, as a result of $y$ being a perfect square.\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\nlog|2007^{2}| = 2log|2007|\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer isboxed$\\boxed{\\boxed{2\\log|2007|}}$\n\n(after you may ask why we put $\\sqrt{4012 \\times 2006}$ into what was $x$, hehe.. I'd be considered a bit dumb lol)",
